<figure id="lock-timeline" class="lock-timeline paper-surface" aria-labelledby="lock-timeline-title">
  <span class="plate-number">Illustration · Row locking</span>
  <h3 id="lock-timeline-title">The second buyer waits before checking</h3>
  <p class="setup">Both transactions want the same product row. There is <strong>1 item</strong> in stock.</p>
  <p class="direction" aria-hidden="true">Time runs down <span>↓</span></p>

  <table aria-hidden="true">
    <thead><tr><th>Buyer 1</th><th>Buyer 2</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td class="event"><strong>Lock the row</strong><span>Lock acquired.</span></td>
        <td class="event"><strong>Start transaction</strong><span>Needs the same row.</span></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="event"><strong>Check stock: 1</strong><span>One item is available.</span></td>
        <td class="event"><strong>Request the lock</strong><span>Buyer 1 still holds it.</span></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="event"><strong>Reserve the item</strong><span>Update stock to 0.</span></td>
        <td class="waiting" rowspan="2"><div><strong>Waiting</strong><span>No stock check yet.</span></div></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="event release"><strong>Commit purchase</strong><span>Release the lock.</span></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="ended"><span>Transaction ended.</span></td>
        <td class="event acquired"><strong>Acquire the lock</strong><span>Check stock: <b>0</b>.</span></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td></td>
        <td class="event finish"><strong>Decline purchase</strong><span>End transaction;<br />release the lock.</span></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <ol class="visually-hidden">
    <li>Buyer 1 locks the product row. Buyer 2 starts a transaction that needs the same row.</li>
    <li>Buyer 1 checks stock and sees one item. Buyer 2 requests the lock and must wait.</li>
    <li>Buyer 1 reserves the item and changes stock to zero. Buyer 2 continues waiting, without checking stock.</li>
    <li>Buyer 1 commits the purchase and releases the lock.</li>
    <li>Buyer 2 acquires the lock and checks the updated stock value: zero.</li>
    <li>Buyer 2 declines the purchase, ends its transaction, and releases the lock.</li>
  </ol>
  <figcaption>The lock protects the stock check as well as the update. This sequence assumes PostgreSQL’s default Read Committed behaviour and a request to lock the row before reading its stock. Spacing shows order, not measured duration; this is an illustration, not a benchmark.</figcaption>
</figure>

